<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Blue Bus.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name common objects and people, including family members. Interactive: Take part in very simple classroom games using one or two repeated lines.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can name transportation vehicles using color words (e.g., "That's a green car."). Can answer simple questions about transportation and colors using one or two words (e.g., "What color is the car?"). Can participate in classroom activities involving transportation vocabulary (e.g., "naming vehicles").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, plays a "Transportation Song" (e.g., "The Wheels on the Bus"), and leads a TPR activity. As the song plays, the teacher shows large flashcards of "car," "bus," "bike," "truck," "train," "taxi." Students repeat the vehicle names. The teacher then quickly flashes color cards (red, blue, green, yellow, orange, purple) and students shout out the colors.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a colored vehicle (e.g., a blue bus). The teacher points and says clearly, "That's a blue bus. It's a blue bus." The teacher repeats this with a red car: "That's a red car. It's a red car." The teacher highlights the /k/ sound in "car," the /b/ sound in "bus," and the /y/ sound in "yellow" as new examples come up. The teacher uses gestures to indicate "that" and "it is." Students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher holds up a green truck flashcard.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it a car or a truck?" (Teacher points to car and truck images.) Expected answer: "Truck!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it blue or green?" (Teacher points to blue and green colors.) Expected answer: "Green!"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors of Things that Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture cards or a simple projected slideshow depicting different colored transportation vehicles (e.g., a green car, a yellow taxi, a purple bike, an orange train). A simple dialogue or question/answer sequence displayed visually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing objects: When describing colors in transportation, use phrases that describe vehicles, like "It is a red bike." "That is a green car." or "It's a blue bus."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a large picture of a scene with various colored vehicles. The teacher points to each vehicle and asks, "What do you see?" (e.g., "a bus!") Then, "What color is the bus?" (e.g., "Blue!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then models full sentences: "It's a blue bus." "That's a green car." Students listen and repeat chorally several times.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the WH-question "What color is the...?" by pointing to a specific vehicle and asking the question, then answering it. (e.g., Teacher: "What color is the bike?" (points to a red bike). Teacher: "It is a red bike.") Students practice repeating the questions and answers chorally.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Point to the yellow taxi!" Students point to the correct vehicle on the big picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows two vehicle pictures (e.g., a red car and a blue car). Teacher asks: "Is this a blue car?" (pointing to the red one). Students respond with gestures (nod for yes, shake head for no) or one word ("No!").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice naming transportation vehicles using color words ("Color + noun," "It is a [color] [vehicle]," "That's a [color] [vehicle]") and answering simple "What color is the...?" questions. Pronunciation focus on /k/, /b/, /y/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Repetition and Naming (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher uses a set of large flashcards depicting different colored vehicles (e.g., a blue bus, a red car, a green truck). The teacher holds up each card and says the full sentence, "That's a blue bus." Students repeat chorally. The teacher repeats with all target vehicles and colors. Then, the teacher holds up a card and asks, "What's this?" and prompts for the full sentence, "It's a blue bus." The teacher specifically exaggerates the initial sounds /k/ in 'car', /b/ in 'bus', and /y/ in 'yellow' during repetition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Question & Answer Chain (Semi-controlled):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by holding a colored vehicle flashcard (e.g., a purple bike) and asks the student next to them, "What color is the bike?" The student answers, "It's a purple bike." Then that student asks the next student, holding up a different flashcard (e.g., an orange train): "What color is the train?" The chain continues around the circle. The teacher provides support and models answers as needed, ensuring polite requests for participation, e.g., "Please, [Student's Name], what color is the train?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Find the Color" Pair Work (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of small colored vehicle picture cards and a mat with outlines of vehicles. One student hides a specific card (e.g., "the yellow taxi") and says, "Please, find the yellow taxi." The other student finds it and says, "Here is the yellow taxi. That's a yellow taxi!" They switch roles. The teacher circulates, listening for correct use of "Color + noun" and polite requests.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors of Things that Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Color and Vehicle Show-and-Tell." Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each student has 2-3 small colored vehicle picture cards (or cutouts they colored themselves). They take turns presenting their cards to their group members.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A shows a card (e.g., a blue bus) and says, "That's a blue bus." or "It is a blue bus."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B (or C) asks, "What color is the bus?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A responds, "It's blue!" or "It's a blue bus."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns showing their cards and asking/answering questions. The teacher circulates, observing fluency, interaction, and correct use of target language. The teacher encourages students to use politeness when asking for a turn or question, e.g., "Please, can I ask?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher may provide sentence frames on the board for support: "That's a _______ ________." "What color is the _________?" "It's ________."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Color and Vehicle Show-and-Tell" activity and during a quick individual check-in.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student is shown 2-3 different colored vehicle flashcards individually by the teacher. For each card, the student must orally:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Name the vehicle and its color using "That's a [color] [vehicle]." (e.g., "That's a green car.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answer the question "What color is the [vehicle]?" with one or two words (e.g., "Red." or "It's red.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of vehicle names and colors, especially /k/, /b/, and /y/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "Color + noun" structure (e.g., "blue bus," "red car").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi; green, blue, red, yellow, purple, orange).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to simple WH-questions about color and vehicles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation in classroom activities.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper with two questions and smiley/sad faces.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say the color and name of a car." (Students circle Yes/No or draw a smiley/sad face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can answer 'What color is it?'" (Students circle Yes/No or draw a smiley/sad face).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> The teacher facilitates a brief class discussion with guiding questions:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today?" (Teacher prompts: "Car, bus, red, blue...")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun about talking about vehicles and colors?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., choral repetition, pair activities, TPR).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in speaking? (e.g., forming full sentences, specific pronunciation sounds). What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., more sentence frames, slower pace, more individual modeling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did the activities ensure students had opportunities to use target grammar (color + noun, present simple, WH-questions) and vocabulary (vehicles, colors) meaningfully?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to confidently name vehicles and their colors. In Lesson 4, students will apply this oral knowledge to writing, forming simple sentences about colored vehicles. They will identify and describe colored vehicles in simple written sentences and match written words to pictures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>